{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21188","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21188","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21188","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiequn","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiequn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhan","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Zhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PLAN: Proactive Low-Rank Allocation for Continual Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T06:37:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:28:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Continual learning (CL) requires models to continuously adapt to new tasks without forgetting past knowledge. In this work, we propose \\underline{P}roactive \\underline{L}ow-rank \\underline{A}llocatio\\underline{N} (PLAN), a framework that extends Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) to enable efficient and interference-aware fine-tuning of large pre-trained models in CL settings. PLAN proactively manages the allocation of task-specific subspaces by introducing orthogonal basis vectors for each task and optimizing them through a perturbation-based strategy that minimizes conflicts with previously learned parameters. Furthermore, PLAN incorporates a novel selection mechanism that identifies and assigns basis vectors with minimal sensitivity to interference, reducing the risk of degrading past knowledge while maintaining efficient adaptation to new tasks. Empirical results on standard CL benchmarks demonstrate that PLAN consistently outperforms existing methods, establishing a new state-of-the-art for continual learning with foundation models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted by ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21188","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:49Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:48:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21184","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21184","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21184","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephen","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Stephen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aidan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Aidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rob","familyName":"Brekelmans","name":"Brekelmans, Rob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roger","familyName":"Grosse","name":"Grosse, Roger","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T06:23:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:27:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has become a predominant technique to align language models (LMs) with human preferences or promote outputs which are deemed to be desirable by a given reward function. Standard RL approaches optimize average reward, while methods explicitly focused on reducing the probability of undesired outputs typically come at a cost to average-case performance. To improve this tradeoff, we introduce RePULSe, a new training method that augments the standard RL loss with an additional loss that uses learned proposals to guide sampling low-reward outputs, and then reduces those outputs' probability. We run experiments demonstrating that RePULSe produces a better tradeoff of expected reward versus the probability of undesired outputs and is more adversarially robust, compared to standard RL alignment approaches and alternatives."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21184","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:43Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:48:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21180","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21180","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21180","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianpei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Xianpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyu","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baolei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Baolei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Social Simulations with Large Language Model Risk Utopian Illusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T06:08:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:27:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reliable simulation of human behavior is essential for explaining, predicting, and intervening in our society. Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have shown promise in emulating human behaviors, interactions, and decision-making, offering a powerful new lens for social science studies. However, the extent to which LLMs diverge from authentic human behavior in social contexts remains underexplored, posing risks of misinterpretation in scientific studies and unintended consequences in real-world applications. Here, we introduce a systematic framework for analyzing LLMs' behavior in social simulation. Our approach simulates multi-agent interactions through chatroom-style conversations and analyzes them across five linguistic dimensions, providing a simple yet effective method to examine emergent social cognitive biases. We conduct extensive experiments involving eight representative LLMs across three families. Our findings reveal that LLMs do not faithfully reproduce genuine human behavior but instead reflect overly idealized versions of it, shaped by the social desirability bias. In particular, LLMs show social role bias, primacy effect, and positivity bias, resulting in \"Utopian\" societies that lack the complexity and variability of real human interactions. These findings call for more socially grounded LLMs that capture the diversity of human social behavior."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21180","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:38Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:48:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21160","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21160","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21160","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanlin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Guanlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyan","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Boyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichen","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao Frank","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hao Frank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Physics-informed Spatial Intelligence with Human Priors: An Autonomous Driving Pilot Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T05:21:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:25:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"How to integrate and verify spatial intelligence in foundation models remains an open challenge. Current practice often proxies Visual-Spatial Intelligence (VSI) with purely textual prompts and VQA-style scoring, which obscures geometry, invites linguistic shortcuts, and weakens attribution to genuinely spatial skills. We introduce Spatial Intelligence Grid (SIG): a structured, grid-based schema that explicitly encodes object layouts, inter-object relations, and physically grounded priors. As a complementary channel to text, SIG provides a faithful, compositional representation of scene structure for foundation-model reasoning. Building on SIG, we derive SIG-informed evaluation metrics that quantify a model's intrinsic VSI, which separates spatial capability from language priors. In few-shot in-context learning with state-of-the-art multimodal LLMs (e.g. GPT- and Gemini-family models), SIG yields consistently larger, more stable, and more comprehensive gains across all VSI metrics compared to VQA-only representations, indicating its promise as a data-labeling and training schema for learning VSI. We also release SIGBench, a benchmark of 1.4K driving frames annotated with ground-truth SIG labels and human gaze traces, supporting both grid-based machine VSI tasks and attention-driven, human-like VSI tasks in autonomous-driving scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 (Spotlight)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21160","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:09Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:48:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21148","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21148","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21148","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao Frank","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hao Frank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How to Auto-optimize Prompts for Domain Tasks? Adaptive Prompting and Reasoning through Evolutionary Domain Knowledge Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T04:38:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:24:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Designing optimal prompts and reasoning processes for large language models (LLMs) on domain-specific tasks is both necessary and challenging in real-world applications. Determining how to integrate domain knowledge, enhance reasoning efficiency, and even provide domain experts with refined knowledge integration hints are particularly crucial yet unresolved tasks. In this research, we propose Evolutionary Graph Optimization for Prompting (EGO-Prompt), an automated framework to designing better prompts, efficient reasoning processes and providing enhanced causal-informed process. EGO-Prompt begins with a general prompt and fault-tolerant initial Semantic Causal Graph (SCG) descriptions, constructed by human experts, which is then automatically refined and optimized to guide LLM reasoning. Recognizing that expert-defined SCGs may be partial or imperfect and that their optimal integration varies across LLMs, EGO-Prompt integrates a novel causal-guided textual gradient process in two steps: first, generating nearly deterministic reasoning guidance from the SCG for each instance, and second, adapting the LLM to effectively utilize the guidance alongside the original input. The iterative optimization algorithm further refines both the SCG and the reasoning mechanism using textual gradients with ground-truth. We tested the framework on real-world public health, transportation and human behavior tasks. EGO-Prompt achieves 7.32%-12.61% higher F1 than cutting-edge methods, and allows small models to reach the performence of larger models at under 20% of the original cost. It also outputs a refined, domain-specific SCG that improves interpretability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21148","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:52Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21133","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21133","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21133","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Divyanshu","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Divyanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nitin Aravind","familyName":"Birur","name":"Birur, Nitin Aravind","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanay","familyName":"Baswa","name":"Baswa, Tanay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sahil","familyName":"Agarwal","name":"Agarwal, Sahil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prashanth","familyName":"Harshangi","name":"Harshangi, Prashanth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantifying CBRN Risk in Frontier Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:55:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:22:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Frontier Large Language Models (LLMs) pose unprecedented dual-use risks through the potential proliferation of chemical, biological, radiological, and nuclear (CBRN) weapons knowledge. We present the first comprehensive evaluation of 10 leading commercial LLMs against both a novel 200-prompt CBRN dataset and a 180-prompt subset of the FORTRESS benchmark, using a rigorous three-tier attack methodology. Our findings expose critical safety vulnerabilities: Deep Inception attacks achieve 86.0\\% success versus 33.8\\% for direct requests, demonstrating superficial filtering mechanisms; Model safety performance varies dramatically from 2\\% (claude-opus-4) to 96\\% (mistral-small-latest) attack success rates; and eight models exceed 70\\% vulnerability when asked to enhance dangerous material properties. We identify fundamental brittleness in current safety alignment, where simple prompt engineering techniques bypass safeguards for dangerous CBRN information. These results challenge industry safety claims and highlight urgent needs for standardized evaluation frameworks, transparent safety metrics, and more robust alignment techniques to mitigate catastrophic misuse risks while preserving beneficial capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21133","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:31Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21131","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21131","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21131","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangxin","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Guangxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanchen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jianwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large Language Models Meet Text-Attributed Graphs: A Survey of Integration Frameworks and Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:53:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:22:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success in natural language processing through strong semantic understanding and generation. However, their black-box nature limits structured and multi-hop reasoning. In contrast, Text-Attributed Graphs (TAGs) provide explicit relational structures enriched with textual context, yet often lack semantic depth. Recent research shows that combining LLMs and TAGs yields complementary benefits: enhancing TAG representation learning and improving the reasoning and interpretability of LLMs. This survey provides the first systematic review of LLM--TAG integration from an orchestration perspective. We introduce a novel taxonomy covering two fundamental directions: LLM for TAG, where LLMs enrich graph-based tasks, and TAG for LLM, where structured graphs improve LLM reasoning. We categorize orchestration strategies into sequential, parallel, and multi-module frameworks, and discuss advances in TAG-specific pretraining, prompting, and parameter-efficient fine-tuning. Beyond methodology, we summarize empirical insights, curate available datasets, and highlight diverse applications across recommendation systems, biomedical analysis, and knowledge-intensive question answering. Finally, we outline open challenges and promising research directions, aiming to guide future work at the intersection of language and graph learning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Surveys and overviews; Natural language processing; Knowledge representation and reasoning; Graph algorithms"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21131","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:28Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21121","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21121","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21121","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibo","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Haibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boce","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Boce","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhe","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ziyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xupeng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xupeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Owen","familyName":"Howell","name":"Howell, Owen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haojie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robin","familyName":"Walters","name":"Walters, Robin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Platt","name":"Platt, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalizable Hierarchical Skill Learning via Object-Centric Representation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:21:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:21:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Generalizable Hierarchical Skill Learning (GSL), a novel framework for hierarchical policy learning that significantly improves policy generalization and sample efficiency in robot manipulation. One core idea of GSL is to use object-centric skills as an interface that bridges the high-level vision-language model and the low-level visual-motor policy. Specifically, GSL decomposes demonstrations into transferable and object-canonicalized skill primitives using foundation models, ensuring efficient low-level skill learning in the object frame. At test time, the skill-object pairs predicted by the high-level agent are fed to the low-level module, where the inferred canonical actions are mapped back to the world frame for execution. This structured yet flexible design leads to substantial improvements in sample efficiency and generalization of our method across unseen spatial arrangements, object appearances, and task compositions. In simulation, GSL trained with only 3 demonstrations per task outperforms baselines trained with 30 times more data by 15.5 percent on unseen tasks. In real-world experiments, GSL also surpasses the baseline trained with 10 times more data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21121","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:14Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21120","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21120","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21120","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alec","familyName":"Helbling","name":"Helbling, Alec","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shruti","familyName":"Palaskar","name":"Palaskar, Shruti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kundan","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Kundan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Polo","familyName":"Chau","name":"Chau, Polo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leon","familyName":"Gatys","name":"Gatys, Leon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph Yitan","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Joseph Yitan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SafetyPairs: Isolating Safety Critical Image Features with Counterfactual Image Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:19:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:21:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"What exactly makes a particular image unsafe? Systematically differentiating between benign and problematic images is a challenging problem, as subtle changes to an image, such as an insulting gesture or symbol, can drastically alter its safety implications. However, existing image safety datasets are coarse and ambiguous, offering only broad safety labels without isolating the specific features that drive these differences. We introduce SafetyPairs, a scalable framework for generating counterfactual pairs of images, that differ only in the features relevant to the given safety policy, thus flipping their safety label. By leveraging image editing models, we make targeted changes to images that alter their safety labels while leaving safety-irrelevant details unchanged. Using SafetyPairs, we construct a new safety benchmark, which serves as a powerful source of evaluation data that highlights weaknesses in vision-language models' abilities to distinguish between subtly different images. Beyond evaluation, we find our pipeline serves as an effective data augmentation strategy that improves the sample efficiency of training lightweight guard models. We release a benchmark containing over 3,020 SafetyPair images spanning a diverse taxonomy of 9 safety categories, providing the first systematic resource for studying fine-grained image safety distinctions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21120","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:12Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21119","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21119","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21119","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Arbour","name":"Arbour, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raghavendra","familyName":"Addanki","name":"Addanki, Raghavendra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ritwik","familyName":"Sinha","name":"Sinha, Ritwik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avi","familyName":"Feller","name":"Feller, Avi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Leveraging semantic similarity for experimentation with AI-generated treatments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62K86, 65F55","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:19:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:21:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) enable a new form of digital experimentation where treatments combine human and model-generated content in increasingly sophisticated ways. The main methodological challenge in this setting is representing these high-dimensional treatments without losing their semantic meaning or rendering analysis intractable. Here, we address this problem by focusing on learning low-dimensional representations that capture the underlying structure of such treatments. These representations enable downstream applications such as guiding generative models to produce meaningful treatment variants and facilitating adaptive assignment in online experiments. We propose double kernel representation learning, which models the causal effect through the inner product of kernel-based representations of treatments and user covariates. We develop an alternating-minimization algorithm that learns these representations efficiently from data and provides convergence guarantees under a low-rank factor model. As an application of this framework, we introduce an adaptive design strategy for online experimentation and demonstrate the method's effectiveness through numerical experiments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21119","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:11Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21115","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21115","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21115","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgios","familyName":"Milis","name":"Milis, Georgios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruibo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ruibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust Distortion-Free Watermark for Autoregressive Audio Generation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:09:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:20:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid advancement of next-token-prediction models has led to widespread adoption across modalities, enabling the creation of realistic synthetic media. In the audio domain, while autoregressive speech models have propelled conversational interactions forward, the potential for misuse, such as impersonation in phishing schemes or crafting misleading speech recordings, has also increased. Security measures such as watermarking have thus become essential to ensuring the authenticity of digital media. Traditional statistical watermarking methods used for autoregressive language models face challenges when applied to autoregressive audio models, due to the inevitable ``retokenization mismatch'' - the discrepancy between original and retokenized discrete audio token sequences. To address this, we introduce Aligned-IS, a novel, distortion-free watermark, specifically crafted for audio generation models. This technique utilizes a clustering approach that treats tokens within the same cluster equivalently, effectively countering the retokenization mismatch issue. Our comprehensive testing on prevalent audio generation platforms demonstrates that Aligned-IS not only preserves the quality of generated audio but also significantly improves the watermark detectability compared to the state-of-the-art distortion-free watermarking adaptations, establishing a new benchmark in secure audio technology applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21115","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:05Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21114","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21114","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21114","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanguang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yanguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Lian","name":"Lian, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Controllable-LPMoE: Adapting to Challenging Object Segmentation via Dynamic Local Priors from Mixture-of-Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:03:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:20:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale foundation models provide powerful feature representations for downstream object segmentation tasks. However, when adapted to specific tasks through the full-parameter fine-tuning, the enormous parameters being updated often results in significant computational overhead, creating a bottleneck in training efficiency. Although existing methods attempt to fine-tune frozen models by directly embedding trainable prompts, these prompts lack inherent semantic priors, limiting the adaptability of large-scale models. In this paper, we propose a novel dynamic priors-based fine-tuning paradigm with fewer trainable parameters, dubbed Controllable-LPMoE, which adaptively modulates frozen foundation models by dynamically controlling local priors to enhance fine-grained perception for specific segmentation tasks. More specifically, we construct a lightweight dynamic mixed local priors extractor that captures diverse local priors from input images through heterogeneous convolutions while employing a gating network to dynamically output expert priors required for the subsequent fine-tuning. Furthermore, we design a bi-directional interaction adapter that employs cosine-aligned deformable attention and channel-oriented adaptive scale enhancement to interact and restructure between frozen and trainable features, achieving efficient fine-tuning. Extensive experiments validate the superiority of our \\href{https://github.com/CSYSI/Controllable-LPMoE} {Controllable-LPMoE} approach, demonstrating excellent segmentation performance compared to 31 state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods and adaptability to multiple binary object segmentation tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21114","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:04Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21111","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21111","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21111","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijie","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Weijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuantang","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Xuantang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manli","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Manli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoyang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Chaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honghui","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Honghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinqiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jinqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PhysVLM-AVR: Active Visual Reasoning for Multimodal Large Language Models in Physical Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T02:59:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:20:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual reasoning in multimodal large language models (MLLMs) has primarily been studied in static, fully observable settings, limiting their effectiveness in real-world environments where information is often incomplete due to occlusion or limited field of view. Humans, in contrast, actively explore and interact with their environment-moving, examining, and manipulating objects-to gather information through a closed-loop process integrating perception, reasoning, and action. Inspired by this human capability, we introduce the Active Visual Reasoning (AVR) task, extending visual reasoning to partially observable, interactive environments. AVR necessitates agents to: (1) actively acquire information via sequential physical actions, (2) integrate observations across multiple steps for coherent reasoning, and (3) dynamically adjust decisions based on evolving visual feedback. To rigorously evaluate AVR, we introduce CLEVR-AVR, a simulation benchmark featuring multi-round interactive environments designed to assess both reasoning correctness and information-gathering efficiency. We present AVR-152k, a large-scale dataset that offers rich Chain-of-Thought (CoT) annotations detailing iterative reasoning for uncertainty identification, action-conditioned information gain prediction, and information-maximizing action selection, crucial for training agents in a higher-order Markov Decision Process. Building on this, we develop PhysVLM-AVR, an MLLM achieving state-of-the-art performance on CLEVR-AVR, embodied reasoning (OpenEQA, RoboVQA), and passive visual reasoning (GeoMath, Geometry30K). Our analysis also reveals that current embodied MLLMs, despite detecting information incompleteness, struggle to actively acquire and integrate new information through interaction, highlighting a fundamental gap in active reasoning capabilities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systemss (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21111","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:00Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21106","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21106","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21106","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacky Wai","familyName":"Keung","name":"Keung, Jacky Wai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pak Yuen Patrick","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Pak Yuen Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicheng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengji","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fengji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"R2ComSync: Improving Code-Comment Synchronization with In-Context Learning and Reranking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T02:48:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:19:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Code-Comment Synchronization (CCS) aims to synchronize the comments with code changes in an automated fashion, thereby significantly reducing the workload of developers during software maintenance and evolution. While previous studies have proposed various solutions that have shown success, they often exhibit limitations, such as a lack of generalization ability or the need for extensive task-specific learning resources. This motivates us to investigate the potential of Large Language Models (LLMs) in this area. However, a pilot analysis proves that LLMs fall short of State-Of-The-Art (SOTA) CCS approaches because (1) they lack instructive demonstrations for In-Context Learning (ICL) and (2) many correct-prone candidates are not prioritized.To tackle the above challenges, we propose R2ComSync, an ICL-based code-Comment Synchronization approach enhanced with Retrieval and Re-ranking. Specifically, R2ComSync carries corresponding two novelties: (1) Ensemble hybrid retrieval. It equally considers the similarity in both code-comment semantics and change patterns when retrieval, thereby creating ICL prompts with effective examples. (2) Multi-turn re-ranking strategy. We derived three significant rules through large-scale CCS sample analysis. Given the inference results of LLMs, it progressively exploits three re-ranking rules to prioritize relatively correct-prone candidates. We evaluate R2ComSync using five recent LLMs on three CCS datasets covering both Java and Python programming languages, and make comparisons with five SOTA approaches. Extensive experiments demonstrate the superior performance of R2ComSync against other approaches. Moreover, both quantitative and qualitative analyses provide compelling evidence that the comments synchronized by our proposal exhibit significantly higher quality.}"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21106","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:53Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21094","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21094","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21094","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wanwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanghaoran","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tanghaoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyu","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longfei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Longfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinjun","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Xinjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuzheng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Shuzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael R.","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Michael R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BDiff: Block-aware and Accurate Text-based Code Differencing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T02:12:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:17:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Code differencing is a fundamental technique in software engineering practice and research. While researchers have proposed text-based differencing techniques capable of identifying line changes over the past decade, existing methods exhibit a notable limitation in identifying edit actions (EAs) that operate on text blocks spanning multiple lines. Such EAs are common in developers' practice, such as moving a code block for conditional branching or duplicating a method definition block for overloading. Existing tools represent such block-level operations as discrete sequences of line-level EAs, compelling developers to manually correlate them and thereby substantially impeding the efficiency of change comprehension. To address this issue, we propose BDiff, a text-based differencing algorithm capable of identifying two types of block-level EAs and five types of line-level EAs. Building on traditional differencing algorithms, we first construct a candidate set containing all possible line mappings and block mappings. Leveraging the Kuhn-Munkres algorithm, we then compute the optimal mapping set that can minimize the size of the edit script (ES) while closely aligning with the original developer's intent. To validate the effectiveness of BDiff, we selected five state-of-the-art tools, including large language models (LLMs), as baselines and adopted a combined qualitative and quantitative approach to evaluate their performance in terms of ES size, result quality, and running time. Experimental results show that BDiff produces higher-quality differencing results than baseline tools while maintaining competitive runtime performance. Our experiments also show the unreliability of LLMs in code differencing tasks regarding result quality and their infeasibility in terms of runtime efficiency. We have implemented a web-based visual differencing tool."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21094","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:36Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21093","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21093","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21093","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Siyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinbo","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Jinbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawen","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Jiawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tenghui","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Tenghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xumin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xumin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanjia","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Yuanjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hudan","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Hudan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zishao","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Zishao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dusit","familyName":"Niyato","name":"Niyato, Dusit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengli","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Shengli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong In","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dong In","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MedAlign: A Synergistic Framework of Multimodal Preference Optimization and Federated Meta-Cognitive Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T02:11:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:17:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently, large models have shown significant potential for smart healthcare. However, the deployment of Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) for clinical services is currently hindered by three critical challenges: a tendency to hallucinate answers not grounded in visual evidence, the inefficiency of fixed-depth reasoning, and the difficulty of multi-institutional collaboration. To address these challenges, in this paper, we develop MedAlign, a novel framework to ensure visually accurate LVLM responses for Medical Visual Question Answering (Med-VQA). Specifically, we first propose a multimodal Direct Preference Optimization (mDPO) objective to explicitly align preference learning with visual context. We then design a Retrieval-Aware Mixture-of-Experts (RA-MoE) architecture that utilizes image and text similarity to route queries to a specialized and context-augmented LVLM (i.e., an expert), thereby mitigating hallucinations in LVLMs. To achieve adaptive reasoning and facilitate multi-institutional collaboration, we propose a federated governance mechanism, where the selected expert, fine-tuned on clinical datasets based on mDPO, locally performs iterative Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning via the local meta-cognitive uncertainty estimator. Extensive experiments on three representative Med-VQA datasets demonstrate that MedAlign achieves state-of-the-art performance, outperforming strong retrieval-augmented baselines by up to $11.85\\%$ in F1-score, and simultaneously reducing the average reasoning length by $51.60\\%$ compared with fixed-depth CoT approaches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21093","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:35Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21090","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21090","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21090","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingru","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qingru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilgee","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Ilgee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenghao","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhenghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongzhi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Rongzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lihong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianshu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jianshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoming","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Haoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuo","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Tuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Rewarding PPO: Aligning Large Language Models with Demonstrations Only"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T02:02:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:17:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Supervised fine-tuning (SFT) has emerged as a crucial method for aligning large language models (LLMs) with human-annotated demonstrations. However, SFT, being an off-policy approach similar to behavior cloning, often struggles with overfitting and poor out-of-domain generalization, especially in limited-data scenarios. To address these limitations, we propose Self-Rewarding PPO, a novel fine-tuning method that leverages on-policy techniques to enhance generalization performance. Our approach combines the strengths of SFT and proximal policy optimization (PPO) to achieve more effective alignment from demonstration data. At its core is a reward function designed as the log policy ratio between the SFT model and the pretrained base model. This function serves as an implicit reward signal, using the pretrained policy as a baseline and the SFT policy as a target. By doing so, it enables on-policy fine-tuning without relying on human preference annotations. The integration of this self-rewarding mechanism with PPO addresses key limitations of SFT, improving generalization, data efficiency, and robustness. Our empirical evaluation across a range of natural language processing tasks demonstrates that Self-Rewarding PPO consistently outperforms traditional SFT methods. The results highlight the effectiveness of our approach in aligning LLMs using demonstration data, particularly in scenarios where high-quality annotated data is scarce."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by COLM 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21090","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:31Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21083","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21083","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21083","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youssef","familyName":"Megahed","name":"Megahed, Youssef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atallah","familyName":"Madi","name":"Madi, Atallah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dina El","familyName":"Demellawy","name":"Demellawy, Dina El","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adrian D. C.","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Adrian D. C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Knowledge-Driven Vision-Language Model for Plexus Detection in Hirschsprung's Disease"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T01:42:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:16:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hirschsprung's disease is defined as the congenital absence of ganglion cells in some segment(s) of the colon. The muscle cannot make coordinated movements to propel stool in that section, most commonly leading to obstruction. The diagnosis and treatment for this disease require a clear identification of different region(s) of the myenteric plexus, where ganglion cells should be present, on the microscopic view of the tissue slide. While deep learning approaches, such as Convolutional Neural Networks, have performed very well in this task, they are often treated as black boxes, with minimal understanding gained from them, and may not conform to how a physician makes decisions. In this study, we propose a novel framework that integrates expert-derived textual concepts into a Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training-based vision-language model to guide plexus classification. Using prompts derived from expert sources (e.g., medical textbooks and papers) generated by large language models and reviewed by our team before being encoded with QuiltNet, our approach aligns clinically relevant semantic cues with visual features. Experimental results show that the proposed model demonstrated superior discriminative capability across different classification metrics as it outperformed CNN-based models, including VGG-19, ResNet-18, and ResNet-50; achieving an accuracy of 83.9%, a precision of 86.6%, and a specificity of 87.6%. These findings highlight the potential of multi-modal learning in histopathology and underscore the value of incorporating expert knowledge for more clinically relevant model outputs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted into the ICAAI 2025 - The 9th International Conference on Advances in Artificial Intelligence"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21083","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:21Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21069","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21069","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21069","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pranav","familyName":"Saxena","name":"Saxena, Pranav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jimmy","familyName":"Chiun","name":"Chiun, Jimmy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ZING-3D: Zero-shot Incremental 3D Scene Graphs via Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T00:52:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:14:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding and reasoning about complex 3D environments requires structured scene representations that capture not only objects but also their semantic and spatial relationships. While recent works on 3D scene graph generation have leveraged pretrained VLMs without task-specific fine-tuning, they are largely confined to single-view settings, fail to support incremental updates as new observations arrive and lack explicit geometric grounding in 3D space, all of which are essential for embodied scenarios. In this paper, we propose, ZING-3D, a framework that leverages the vast knowledge of pretrained foundation models to enable open-vocabulary recognition and generate a rich semantic representation of the scene in a zero-shot manner while also enabling incremental updates and geometric grounding in 3D space, making it suitable for downstream robotics applications. Our approach leverages VLM reasoning to generate a rich 2D scene graph, which is grounded in 3D using depth information. Nodes represent open-vocabulary objects with features, 3D locations, and semantic context, while edges capture spatial and semantic relations with inter-object distances. Our experiments on scenes from the Replica and HM3D dataset show that ZING-3D is effective at capturing spatial and relational knowledge without the need of task-specific training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21069","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:00Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21067","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21067","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21067","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raul Cavalcante","familyName":"Dinardi","name":"Dinardi, Raul Cavalcante","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruno","familyName":"Yamamoto","name":"Yamamoto, Bruno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna Helena Reali","familyName":"Costa","name":"Costa, Anna Helena Reali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Artur","familyName":"Jordao","name":"Jordao, Artur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Virtues of Brevity: Avoid Overthinking in Parallel Test-Time Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T00:47:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:14:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning models represent a significant advance in LLM capabilities, particularly for complex reasoning tasks such as mathematics and coding. Previous studies confirm that parallel test-time compute-sampling multiple solutions and selecting the best one-can further enhance the predictive performance of LLMs. However, strategies in this area often require complex scoring, thus increasing computational cost and complexity. In this work, we demonstrate that the simple and counterintuitive heuristic of selecting the shortest solution is highly effective. We posit that the observed effectiveness stems from models operating in two distinct regimes: a concise, confident conventional regime and a verbose overthinking regime characterized by uncertainty, and we show evidence of a critical point where the overthinking regime begins to be significant. By selecting the shortest answer, the heuristic preferentially samples from the conventional regime. We confirm that this approach is competitive with more complex methods such as self-consistency across two challenging benchmarks while significantly reducing computational overhead. The shortest-answer heuristic provides a Pareto improvement over self-consistency and applies even to tasks where output equality is not well defined."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Efficient Reasoning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21067","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:45:58Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:45:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:45:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21053","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21053","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21053","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"An","name":"An, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yujian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yepeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yepeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuheng","familyName":"Bu","name":"Bu, Yuheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Reinforcement Learning Framework for Robust and Secure LLM Watermarking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T23:53:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:12:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Watermarking has emerged as a promising solution for tracing and authenticating text generated by large language models (LLMs). A common approach to LLM watermarking is to construct a green/red token list and assign higher or lower generation probabilities to the corresponding tokens, respectively. However, most existing watermarking algorithms rely on heuristic green/red token list designs, as directly optimizing the list design with techniques such as reinforcement learning (RL) comes with several challenges. First, desirable watermarking involves multiple criteria, i.e., detectability, text quality, robustness against removal attacks, and security against spoofing attacks. Directly optimizing for these criteria introduces many partially conflicting reward terms, leading to an unstable convergence process. Second, the vast action space of green/red token list choices is susceptible to reward hacking. In this paper, we propose an end-to-end RL framework for robust and secure LLM watermarking. Our approach adopts an anchoring mechanism for reward terms to ensure stable training and introduces additional regularization terms to prevent reward hacking. Experiments on standard benchmarks with two backbone LLMs show that our method achieves a state-of-the-art trade-off across all criteria, with notable improvements in resistance to spoofing attacks without degrading other criteria. Our code is available at https://github.com/UCSB-NLP-Chang/RL-watermark."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21053","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:45:38Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:45:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:45:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21049","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21049","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21049","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atoosa","familyName":"Chegini","name":"Chegini, Atoosa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Kazemi","name":"Kazemi, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Garrett","familyName":"Souza","name":"Souza, Garrett","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria","familyName":"Safi","name":"Safi, Maria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samy","familyName":"Bengio","name":"Bengio, Samy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sinead","familyName":"Williamson","name":"Williamson, Sinead","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehrdad","familyName":"Farajtabar","name":"Farajtabar, Mehrdad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning's Razor: Reasoning Improves Accuracy but Can Hurt Recall at Critical Operating Points in Safety and Hallucination Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T23:23:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:12:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning has become a central paradigm for large language models (LLMs), consistently boosting accuracy across diverse benchmarks. Yet its suitability for precision-sensitive tasks remains unclear. We present the first systematic study of reasoning for classification tasks under strict low false positive rate (FPR) regimes. Our analysis covers two tasks--safety detection and hallucination detection--evaluated in both fine-tuned and zero-shot settings, using standard LLMs and Large Reasoning Models (LRMs). Our results reveal a clear trade-off: Think On (reasoning-augmented) generation improves overall accuracy, but underperforms at the low-FPR thresholds essential for practical use. In contrast, Think Off (no reasoning during inference) dominates in these precision-sensitive regimes, with Think On surpassing only when higher FPRs are acceptable. In addition, we find token-based scoring substantially outperforms self-verbalized confidence for precision-sensitive deployments. Finally, a simple ensemble of the two modes recovers the strengths of each. Taken together, our findings position reasoning as a double-edged tool: beneficial for average accuracy, but often ill-suited for applications requiring strict precision."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21049","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:45:32Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:45:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:45:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21031","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21031","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21031","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghua","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Qinghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dehai","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Dehai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liming","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Liming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiwei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angela","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Angela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tristan","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Tristan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rick","familyName":"Kazman","name":"Kazman, Rick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentArcEval: An Architecture Evaluation Method for Foundation Model based Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T22:32:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:10:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.jss.2025.112656","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The emergence of foundation models (FMs) has enabled the development of highly capable and autonomous agents, unlocking new application opportunities across a wide range of domains. Evaluating the architecture of agents is particularly important as the architectural decisions significantly impact the quality attributes of agents given their unique characteristics, including compound architecture, autonomous and non-deterministic behaviour, and continuous evolution. However, these traditional methods fall short in addressing the evaluation needs of agent architecture due to the unique characteristics of these agents. Therefore, in this paper, we present AgentArcEval, a novel agent architecture evaluation method designed specially to address the complexities of FM-based agent architecture and its evaluation. Moreover, we present a catalogue of agent-specific general scenarios, which serves as a guide for generating concrete scenarios to design and evaluate the agent architecture. We demonstrate the usefulness of AgentArcEval and the catalogue through a case study on the architecture evaluation of a real-world tax copilot, named Luna."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21031","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:45:07Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:45:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:45:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21027","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21027","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21027","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Fei","name":"Fei, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehmet Yigit","familyName":"Turali","name":"Turali, Mehmet Yigit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreyas","familyName":"Rajesh","name":"Rajesh, Shreyas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyang","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Xinyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huyen","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Huyen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pavan","familyName":"Holur","name":"Holur, Pavan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhui","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Larissa","familyName":"Mooney","name":"Mooney, Larissa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yih-Ing","familyName":"Hser","name":"Hser, Yih-Ing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vwani","familyName":"Roychowdhury","name":"Roychowdhury, Vwani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Customizing Open Source LLMs for Quantitative Medication Attribute Extraction across Heterogeneous EHR Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T22:27:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:10:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Harmonizing medication data across Electronic Health Record (EHR) systems is a persistent barrier to monitoring medications for opioid use disorder (MOUD). In heterogeneous EHR systems, key prescription attributes are scattered across differently formatted fields and freetext notes. We present a practical framework that customizes open source large language models (LLMs), including Llama, Qwen, Gemma, and MedGemma, to extract a unified set of MOUD prescription attributes (prescription date, drug name, duration, total quantity, daily quantity, and refills) from heterogeneous, site specific data and compute a standardized metric of medication coverage, \\emph{MOUD days}, per patient. Our pipeline processes records directly in a fixed JSON schema, followed by lightweight normalization and cross-field consistency checks. We evaluate the system on prescription level EHR data from five clinics in a national OUD study (25{,}605 records from 1{,}257 patients), using a previously annotated benchmark of 10{,}369 records (776 patients) as the ground truth. Performance is reported as coverage (share of records with a valid, matchable output) and record-level exact-match accuracy. Larger models perform best overall: Qwen2.5-32B achieves \\textbf{93.4\\%} coverage with \\textbf{93.0\\%} exact-match accuracy across clinics, and MedGemma-27B attains \\textbf{93.1\\%}/\\textbf{92.2\\%}. A brief error review highlights three common issues and fixes: imputing missing dosage fields using within-drug norms, handling monthly/weekly injectables (e.g., Vivitrol) by setting duration from the documented schedule, and adding unit checks to prevent mass units (e.g., ``250 g'') from being misread as daily counts. By removing brittle, site-specific ETL and supporting local, privacy-preserving deployment, this approach enables consistent cross-site analyses of MOUD exposure, adherence, and retention in real-world settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025: The Second Workshop on GenAI for Health: Potential, Trust, and Policy Compliance"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21027","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:45:02Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:45:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:45:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21000","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21000","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21000","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongli","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hongli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junwen","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Junwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter KT","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Peter KT","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Slobodan","familyName":"Ilic","name":"Ilic, Slobodan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Busam","name":"Busam, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BioDet: Boosting Industrial Object Detection with Image Preprocessing Strategies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T21:15:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:08:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate 6D pose estimation is essential for robotic manipulation in industrial environments. Existing pipelines typically rely on off-the-shelf object detectors followed by cropping and pose refinement, but their performance degrades under challenging conditions such as clutter, poor lighting, and complex backgrounds, making detection the critical bottleneck. In this work, we introduce a standardized and plug-in pipeline for 2D detection of unseen objects in industrial settings. Based on current SOTA baselines, our approach reduces domain shift and background artifacts through low-light image enhancement and background removal guided by open-vocabulary detection with foundation models. This design suppresses the false positives prevalent in raw SAM outputs, yielding more reliable detections for downstream pose estimation. Extensive experiments on real-world industrial bin-picking benchmarks from BOP demonstrate that our method significantly boosts detection accuracy while incurring negligible inference overhead, showing the effectiveness and practicality of the proposed method."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, accepted by ICCV 2025 R6D"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21000","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:44:25Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:44:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:44:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20994","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20994","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20994","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesimon","familyName":"Barreto","name":"Barreto, Jesimon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlos","familyName":"Caetano","name":"Caetano, Carlos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"André","familyName":"Araujo","name":"Araujo, André","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William Robson","familyName":"Schwartz","name":"Schwartz, William Robson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VESSA: Video-based objEct-centric Self-Supervised Adaptation for Visual Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T20:44:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:07:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models have advanced computer vision by enabling strong performance across diverse tasks through large-scale pretraining and supervised fine-tuning. However, they may underperform in domains with distribution shifts and scarce labels, where supervised fine-tuning may be infeasible. While continued self-supervised learning for model adaptation is common for generative language models, this strategy has not proven effective for vision-centric encoder models. To address this challenge, we introduce a novel formulation of self-supervised fine-tuning for vision foundation models, where the model is adapted to a new domain without requiring annotations, leveraging only short multi-view object-centric videos. Our method is referred to as VESSA: Video-based objEct-centric Self-Supervised Adaptation for visual foundation models. VESSA's training technique is based on a self-distillation paradigm, where it is critical to carefully tune prediction heads and deploy parameter-efficient adaptation techniques - otherwise, the model may quickly forget its pretrained knowledge and reach a degraded state. VESSA benefits significantly from multi-view object observations sourced from different frames in an object-centric video, efficiently learning robustness to varied capture conditions, without the need of annotations. Through comprehensive experiments with 3 vision foundation models on 2 datasets, VESSA demonstrates consistent improvements in downstream classification tasks, compared to the base models and previous adaptation methods. Code is publicly available at https://github.com/jesimonbarreto/VESSA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20994","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:44:17Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:44:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:44:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20976","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20976","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20976","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiyu","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Jiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Fang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haokai","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Haokai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minggao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Minggao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xenophon","familyName":"Evangelopoulos","name":"Evangelopoulos, Xenophon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew I.","familyName":"Cooper","name":"Cooper, Andrew I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yejin","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Yejin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"L^2M^3OF: A Large Language Multimodal Model for Metal-Organic Frameworks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T20:12:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:06:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have demonstrated remarkable reasoning capabilities across diverse natural language tasks. However, comparable breakthroughs in scientific discovery are more limited, because understanding complex physical phenomena demands multifaceted representations far beyond language alone. A compelling example is the design of functional materials such as MOFs-critical for a range of impactful applications like carbon capture and hydrogen storage. Navigating their vast and intricate design space in language-based representations interpretable by LLMs is challenging due to the numerous possible three-dimensional atomic arrangements and strict reticular rules of coordination geometry and topology. Despite promising early results in LLM-assisted discovery for simpler materials systems, MOF design remains heavily reliant on tacit human expertise rarely codified in textual information alone. To overcome this barrier, we introduce L2M3OF, the first multimodal LLM for MOFs. L2M3OF integrates crystal representation learning with language understanding to process structural, textual, and knowledge modalities jointly. L2M3OF employs a pre-trained crystal encoder with a lightweight projection layer to compress structural information into a token space, enabling efficient alignment with language instructions. To facilitate training and evaluation, we curate a structure-property-knowledge database of crystalline materials and benchmark L2M3OF against state-of-the-art closed-source LLMs such as GPT-5, Gemini-2.5-Pro and DeepSeek-R1. Experiments show that L2M3OF outperforms leading text-based closed-source LLMs in property prediction and knowledge generation tasks, despite using far fewer parameters. These results highlight the importance of multimodal approaches for porous material understanding and establish L2M3OF as a foundation for next-generation AI systems in materials discovery."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20976","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:52Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:43:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20967","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20967","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20967","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sraavya","familyName":"Sambara","name":"Sambara, Sraavya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sung Eun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sung Eun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoman","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luyang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Luyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreya","familyName":"Johri","name":"Johri, Shreya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed","familyName":"Baharoon","name":"Baharoon, Mohammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Du Hyun","familyName":"Ro","name":"Ro, Du Hyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pranav","familyName":"Rajpurkar","name":"Rajpurkar, Pranav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"3DReasonKnee: Advancing Grounded Reasoning in Medical Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T19:54:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:06:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current Vision-Language Models (VLMs) struggle to ground anatomical regions in 3D medical images and reason about them in a step-by-step manner, a key requirement of real-world diagnostic assessment. This ability is essential for aligning model outputs with the diagnostic workflows clinicians use in practice, enabling trustworthy clinician-AI collaboration. Existing 3D datasets provide localization labels, but none support this \"grounded reasoning\" ability. To address this gap, we introduce 3DReasonKnee, the first 3D grounded reasoning dataset for medical images, which provides 494k high-quality quintuples derived from 7,970 3D knee MRI volumes. Each quintuple includes: (1) the 3D MRI volume, (2) a diagnostic question targeting a specific anatomical region (3) a 3D bounding box localizing the relevant anatomical structures, (4) clinician-generated diagnostic reasoning steps that explicitly detail the 3D reasoning process, and (5) structured severity assessments for the relevant anatomical region. The creation and validation of 3DReasonKnee, involving over 450 hours of expert clinician time for manually segmenting MRIs and generating reasoning chains, ensures its superior quality and clinical relevance. We establish ReasonKnee-Bench to evaluate localization and diagnostic accuracy, providing insight into VLM ability to perform grounding and severity assessment across anatomical regions and diagnostic inquiries. We benchmark five state-of-the-art VLMs, providing baseline performance for ReasonKnee-Bench. By providing this unique resource of expert-annotated 3D reasoning pathways, 3DReasonKnee serves as a repository of orthopedic surgeons' diagnostic expertise and offers a vital testbed for advancing multimodal medical AI systems towards 3D, clinically aligned, localized decision-making capabilities. The dataset can be found in: https://huggingface.co/datasets/rajpurkarlab/3DReasonKnee"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20967","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:39Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:43:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20957","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20957","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20957","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josh","familyName":"McGiff","name":"McGiff, Josh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khanh-Tung","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Khanh-Tung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Mulcahy","name":"Mulcahy, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dáibhidh Ó","familyName":"Luinín","name":"Luinín, Dáibhidh Ó","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jake","familyName":"Dalzell","name":"Dalzell, Jake","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Róisín Ní","familyName":"Bhroin","name":"Bhroin, Róisín Ní","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Burke","name":"Burke, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Barry","familyName":"O'Sullivan","name":"O'Sullivan, Barry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoang D.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Hoang D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikola S.","familyName":"Nikolov","name":"Nikolov, Nikola S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Irish-BLiMP: A Linguistic Benchmark for Evaluating Human and Language Model Performance in a Low-Resource Setting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T19:36:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:05:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Irish-BLiMP (Irish Benchmark of Linguistic Minimal Pairs), the first dataset and framework designed for fine-grained evaluation of linguistic competence in the Irish language, an endangered language. Drawing on a variety of linguistic literature and grammar reference works, we manually constructed and reviewed 1020 minimal pairs across a taxonomy of 11 linguistic features, through a team of fluent Irish speakers. We evaluate both existing Large Language Models (LLMs) and fluent human participants on their syntactic knowledge of Irish. Our findings show that humans outperform all models across all linguistic features, achieving 16.6% higher accuracy on average. Moreover, a substantial performance gap of 18.1% persists between open- and closed-source LLMs, with even the strongest model (gpt-5) reaching only 73.5% accuracy compared to 90.1% by human. Interestingly, human participants and models struggle on different aspects of Irish grammar, thus highlighting a difference in representation learned by the models. Overall, Irish-BLiMP provides the first systematic framework for evaluating the grammatical competence of LLMs in Irish and offers a valuable benchmark for advancing research on linguistic understanding in low-resource languages."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20957","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:25Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:43:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20952","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20952","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20952","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungjun","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Sungjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changho","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Changho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suenggwan","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Suenggwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinya","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Xinya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shourjo Aditya","familyName":"Chaudhuri","name":"Chaudhuri, Shourjo Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frederic","familyName":"Sala","name":"Sala, Frederic","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Integrated Bayesian State Space Models for Multimodal Time-Series Forecasting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T19:28:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:05:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Forecasting in the real world requires integrating structured time-series data with unstructured textual information, but existing methods are architecturally limited by fixed input/output horizons and are unable to model or quantify uncertainty. We address this challenge by introducing LLM-integrated Bayesian State space models (LBS), a novel probabilistic framework for multimodal temporal forecasting. At a high level, LBS consists of two components: (1) a state space model (SSM) backbone that captures the temporal dynamics of latent states from which both numerical and textual observations are generated and (2) a pretrained large language model (LLM) that is adapted to encode textual inputs for posterior state estimation and decode textual forecasts consistent with the latent trajectory. This design enables flexible lookback and forecast windows, principled uncertainty quantification, and improved temporal generalization thanks to the well-suited inductive bias of SSMs toward modeling dynamical systems. Experiments on the TextTimeCorpus benchmark demonstrate that LBS improves the previous state-of-the-art by 13.20% while providing human-readable summaries of each forecast. Our work is the first to unify LLMs and SSMs for joint numerical and textual prediction, offering a novel foundation for multimodal temporal reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20952","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:18Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:43:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20930","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20930","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20930","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soham","familyName":"Hans","name":"Hans, Soham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stacy","familyName":"Marsella","name":"Marsella, Stacy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sophia","familyName":"Hirschmann","name":"Hirschmann, Sophia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolos","familyName":"Gurney","name":"Gurney, Nikolos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Security Logs to ATT\u0026amp;CK Insights: Leveraging LLMs for High-Level Threat Understanding and Cognitive Trait Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T18:43:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:03:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding adversarial behavior in cybersecurity has traditionally relied on high-level intelligence reports and manual interpretation of attack chains. However, real-time defense requires the ability to infer attacker intent and cognitive strategy directly from low-level system telemetry such as intrusion detection system (IDS) logs. In this paper, we propose a novel framework that leverages large language models (LLMs) to analyze Suricata IDS logs and infer attacker actions in terms of MITRE ATT\u0026amp;CK techniques. Our approach is grounded in the hypothesis that attacker behavior reflects underlying cognitive biases such as loss aversion, risk tolerance, or goal persistence that can be extracted and modeled through careful observation of log sequences. This lays the groundwork for future work on behaviorally adaptive cyber defense and cognitive trait inference. We develop a strategy-driven prompt system to segment large amounts of network logs data into distinct behavioral phases in a highly efficient manner, enabling the LLM to associate each phase with likely techniques and underlying cognitive motives. By mapping network-layer events to high-level attacker strategies, our method reveals how behavioral signals such as tool switching, protocol transitions, or pivot patterns correspond to psychologically meaningful decision points. The results demonstrate that LLMs can bridge the semantic gap between packet-level logs and strategic intent, offering a pathway toward cognitive-adaptive cyber defense.\n Keywords: Cognitive Cybersecurity, Large Language Models (LLMs), Cyberpsychology, Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS), MITRE ATT\u0026amp;CK, Cognitive Biases"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20930","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:42:47Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:42:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:42:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20928","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20928","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20928","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenghao","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Zhenghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Arbour","name":"Arbour, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avi","familyName":"Feller","name":"Feller, Avi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ishita","familyName":"Dasgupta","name":"Dasgupta, Ishita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atanu R","familyName":"Sinha","name":"Sinha, Atanu R","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward H.","familyName":"Kennedy","name":"Kennedy, Edward H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Handling Missing Responses under Cluster Dependence with Applications to Language Model Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T18:38:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:03:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human annotations play a crucial role in evaluating the performance of GenAI models. Two common challenges in practice, however, are missing annotations (the response variable of interest) and cluster dependence among human-AI interactions (e.g., questions asked by the same user may be highly correlated). Reliable inference must address both these issues to achieve unbiased estimation and appropriately quantify uncertainty when estimating average scores from human annotations. In this paper, we analyze the doubly robust estimator, a widely used method in missing data analysis and causal inference, applied to this setting and establish novel theoretical properties under cluster dependence. We further illustrate our findings through simulations and a real-world conversation quality dataset. Our theoretical and empirical results underscore the importance of incorporating cluster dependence in missing response problems to perform valid statistical inference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20928","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:42:44Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:42:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:42:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20909","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20909","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20909","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cedegao E.","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Cedegao E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cédric","familyName":"Colas","name":"Colas, Cédric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel","familyName":"Poesia","name":"Poesia, Gabriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joshua B.","familyName":"Tenenbaum","name":"Tenenbaum, Joshua B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Andreas","name":"Andreas, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Code-enabled language models can outperform reasoning models on diverse tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T18:04:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:02:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning models (RMs), language models (LMs) trained with reinforcement learning to produce long-form natural language reasoning, have been remarkably successful, but they still require large amounts of computation and data to train, and can be slow and expensive to run. In this paper, we show that standard instruct LMs can already be elicited to be strong reasoners at a level comparable to or even surpassing their corresponding RMs (e.g., DeepSeek V3 vs R1) without finetuning, across diverse domains from instruction following and creative generation to mathematical reasoning. This is achieved by CodeAdapt, our simple recipe that combines the CodeAct framework, where LMs interleave natural language reasoning with code execution in a multi-step fashion, with few-shot bootstrap in-context learning from as few as five training problems. Analyzing four matched pairs of LMs and RMs, we find that CodeAdapt enables three LMs to outperform the corresponding RMs on average over eight tasks (up to 22.9%) while being 10-81% more token efficient, and delivers superior performance on six tasks when averaged over the four models (up to 35.7%). Furthermore, the code-augmented reasoning traces display rich and varied problem-solving strategies. Our findings support that (1) CodeAdapt-style learning and reasoning may be robust and domain general and (2) code-enabled LMs are cognitively grounded and powerful systems, potentially providing a strong foundation for in-weight reinforcement learning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20909","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:42:17Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:42:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:42:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20878","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20878","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20878","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danying","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Danying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianhua","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jianhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixue","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yixue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weixing","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Weixing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HA-RAG: Hotness-Aware RAG Acceleration via Mixed Precision and Data Placement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"C.4; E.4; I.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T12:28:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:01:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) improves model output accuracy by leveraging external knowledge bases, serving as an effective solution to address hallucination issues and knowledge-update delays in Large Language Models (LLMs). However, the introduction of external knowledge bases presents RAG with challenges in long-context processing, significantly increasing memory consumption and inference latency. Existing research accelerates inference by precomputing Key and Value (KV) of the knowledge base and loading them on-demand during inference. Based on the access frequency of different KV chunks within the external knowledge base, this paper proposes a hotness-aware RAG (HA-RAG) inference optimization system. First, leveraging the numerical distribution of KV chunks, we introduce a hotness-aware mixed-precision compressing and loading method to reduce disk I/O and memory access overhead. Second, we design a hotness-aware data placement strategy that prioritizes storing frequently accessed KV chunks in high-speed memory to improve data access efficiency. Experimental results demonstrate that, compared with TurboRAG, the proposed HA-RAG achieves an average speedup of 2.10x and maximum speedup of 10.49x in Time-To-First-Token (TTFT) with negligible accuracy loss."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages,16 figures,2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20878","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:41:32Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:41:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:41:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20867","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20867","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20867","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roger","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Roger","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Pandey","name":"Pandey, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prashanth Gurunath","familyName":"Shivakumar","name":"Shivakumar, Prashanth Gurunath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivan","familyName":"Bulyko","name":"Bulyko, Ivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ankur","familyName":"Gandhe","name":"Gandhe, Ankur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yile","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Yile","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Incentivizing Consistent, Effective and Scalable Reasoning Capability in Audio LLMs via Reasoning Process Rewards"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T06:18:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:01:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The role of reasoning in Audio Large Language Models remains widely underexplored, as introducing a reasoning process often degrades rather than improves performance during inference, a phenomenon we term test-time inverse scaling, where longer reasoning chains yield progressively worse results. We demonstrate that this stems not from fundamental limitations of reasoning itself, but from inadequate training: models without proper guidance for the reasoning process produce hallucinatory, inconsistent reasoning that accumulates errors over longer chains. To address these challenges, we introduce CESAR (Consistent, Effective, and Scalable Audio Reasoners), shifting from outcome verification to rewarding the reasoning process. Our online reinforcement learning framework employs Group Relative Policy Optimization with a multi-faceted reward suite that incentivizes not only correctness and format but also consistency, structured analytical patterns, causal reasoning, domain-knowledge integration, and calibrated reasoning depth. CESAR resolves test-time inverse scaling, transforming reasoning from detriments into gains while revealing model-specific ``reasoning sweet spots\", where performance peaks during test-time scaling. We achieve state-of-the-art results on MMAU Test-mini, substantially outperforming Gemini 2.5 Pro and GPT-4o Audio, and near-human-level performance on MMSU reasoning tasks. Through AI-as-judge evaluations and qualitative comparisons, we provide both quantitative and qualitative validation of our improved reasoning quality. Importantly, enhanced reasoning creates synergistic effects, simultaneously improving multimodal reasoning and perception capabilities. Overall, CESAR establishes a principled method for developing robust and scalable reasoning in Audio LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"49 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20867","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:41:17Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:41:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:41:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20860","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20860","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20860","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishaal","familyName":"Udandarao","name":"Udandarao, Vishaal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyun","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zhiyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuankai","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Xuankai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongqiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yongqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Violet Z.","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Violet Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Albin Madapally","familyName":"Jose","name":"Jose, Albin Madapally","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fartash","familyName":"Faghri","name":"Faghri, Fartash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josh","familyName":"Gardner","name":"Gardner, Josh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chung-Cheng","familyName":"Chiu","name":"Chiu, Chung-Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data-Centric Lessons To Improve Speech-Language Pretraining"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T17:34:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:00:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spoken Question-Answering (SQA) is a core capability for useful and interactive artificial intelligence systems. Recently, several speech-language models (SpeechLMs) have been released with a specific focus on improving their SQA performance. However, a lack of controlled ablations of pretraining data processing and curation makes it challenging to understand what factors account for performance, despite substantial gains from similar studies in other data modalities. In this work, we address this gap by conducting a data-centric exploration for pretraining SpeechLMs. We focus on three research questions fundamental to speech-language pretraining data: (1) how to process raw web-crawled audio content for speech-text pretraining, (2) how to construct synthetic pretraining datasets to augment web-crawled data and (3) how to interleave (text, audio) segments into training sequences. We apply the insights from our controlled data-centric ablations to pretrain a 3.8B-parameter SpeechLM, called SpeLangy, that outperforms models that are up to 3x larger by 10.2% absolute performance. We hope our findings highlight the impact of effective data curation for speech-language pretraining and guide future data-centric exploration in SpeechLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Tech Report"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20860","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:41:07Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:41:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:41:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20859","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20859","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20859","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Craig","familyName":"Sanders","name":"Sanders, Craig","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Billy","familyName":"Dickson","name":"Dickson, Billy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sahaj Singh","familyName":"Maini","name":"Maini, Sahaj Singh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Nosofsky","name":"Nosofsky, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zoran","familyName":"Tiganj","name":"Tiganj, Zoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vision-language models learn the geometry of human perceptual space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T16:58:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:00:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In cognitive science and AI, a longstanding question is whether machines learn representations that align with those of the human mind. While current models show promise, it remains an open question whether this alignment is superficial or reflects a deeper correspondence in the underlying dimensions of representation. Here we introduce a methodology to probe the internal geometry of vision-language models (VLMs) by having them generate pairwise similarity judgments for a complex set of natural objects. Using multidimensional scaling, we recover low-dimensional psychological spaces and find that their axes show a strong correspondence with the principal axes of human perceptual space. Critically, when this AI-derived representational geometry is used as the input to a classic exemplar model of categorization, it predicts human classification behavior more accurately than a space constructed from human judgments themselves. This suggests that VLMs can capture an idealized or `denoised' form of human perceptual structure. Our work provides a scalable method to overcome a measurement bottleneck in cognitive science and demonstrates that foundation models can learn a representational geometry that is functionally relevant for modeling key aspects of human cognition, such as categorization."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20859","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:41:05Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:41:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:41:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20856","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20856","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20856","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhua","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaowei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FPT-Noise: Dynamic Scene-Aware Counterattack for Test-Time Adversarial Defense in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T08:29:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:00:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs), such as CLIP, have demonstrated remarkable zero-shot generalizability across diverse downstream tasks. However, recent studies have revealed that VLMs, including CLIP, are highly vulnerable to adversarial attacks, particularly on their visual modality. Traditional methods for improving adversarial robustness, such as adversarial training, involve extensive retraining and can be computationally expensive. In this paper, we propose a new Test-Time defense: Feature Perception Threshold Counterattack Noise (FPT-Noise), which enhances the adversarial robustness of CLIP without costly fine-tuning. Our core contributions are threefold: First, we introduce a Dynamic Feature Modulator that dynamically generate an image-specific and attack-adaptive noise intensity parameter. Second, We reanalyzed the image features of CLIP. When images are exposed to different levels of noise, clean images and adversarial images exhibit distinct rates of feature change. We established a feature perception threshold to distinguish clean images from attacked ones. Finally, we integrate a Scene-Aware Regulation guided by a stability threshold and leverage Test-Time Transformation Ensembling (TTE) to further mitigate the impact of residual noise and enhance robustness.Extensive experimentation has demonstrated that FPT-Noise significantly outperforms existing Test-Time defense methods, boosting average robust accuracy from 0.07% to 56.86% under AutoAttack while maintaining high performance on clean images (-1.1%). The code will be made public following the publication of the study. The code will be made public following the publication of the study."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11pages,4figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20856","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:41:01Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:41:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:41:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20850","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20850","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20850","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chibuzor","familyName":"Okocha","name":"Okocha, Chibuzor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maya","familyName":"Bakri","name":"Bakri, Maya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christan","familyName":"Grant","name":"Grant, Christan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can large audio language models understand child stuttering speech? speech summarization, and source separation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-21T18:53:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:00:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Child speech differs from adult speech in acoustics, prosody, and language development, and disfluencies (repetitions, prolongations, blocks) further challenge Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) and downstream Natural Language Processing (NLP). Recent large audio-language models (LALMs) demonstrate strong cross-modal audio understanding; however, their behavior in disfluent child speech remains underexplored. We evaluate several state-of-the-art LALMs in two settings: an interview (mixed speakers) and a reading task (single child). The tasks are (i) single-channel source separation to isolate the child and (ii) child-only summarization that preserves clinically relevant disfluencies and avoids adult-speech leakage.\n Evaluation combines Large Language Model (LLM) as a judge, human expert ratings, and BERTScore (F1), and we report agreement between models and between models and humans to assess reliability. Our findings delineate the conditions under which LALMs produce faithful child-only summaries from mixed audio and where they fail, offering practical guidance for clinical and educational deployments. We provide prompts and evaluation scripts to support replication."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 1 Figure, 8 tables, Under review ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20850","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:40:53Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:40:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:40:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20849","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20849","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20849","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandro H.","familyName":"Artiles","name":"Artiles, Alejandro H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiromu","familyName":"Yakura","name":"Yakura, Hiromu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Levin","familyName":"Brinkmann","name":"Brinkmann, Levin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mar Canet","familyName":"Sola","name":"Sola, Mar Canet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassan Abu","familyName":"Alhaija","name":"Alhaija, Hassan Abu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ignacio","familyName":"Serna","name":"Serna, Ignacio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nasim","familyName":"Rahaman","name":"Rahaman, Nasim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernhard","familyName":"Schölkopf","name":"Schölkopf, Bernhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iyad","familyName":"Rahwan","name":"Rahwan, Iyad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cultural Alien Sampler: Open-ended art generation balancing originality and coherence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-21T09:32:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:00:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In open-ended domains like art, autonomous agents must generate ideas that are both original and internally coherent, yet current Large Language Models (LLMs) either default to familiar cultural patterns or sacrifice coherence when pushed toward novelty. We address this by introducing the Cultural Alien Sampler (CAS), a concept-selection method that explicitly separates compositional fit from cultural typicality. CAS uses two GPT-2 models fine-tuned on WikiArt concepts: a Concept Coherence Model that scores whether concepts plausibly co-occur within artworks, and a Cultural Context Model that estimates how typical those combinations are within individual artists' bodies of work. CAS targets combinations that are high in coherence and low in typicality, yielding ideas that maintain internal consistency while deviating from learned conventions and embedded cultural context. In a human evaluation (N = 100), our approach outperforms random selection and GPT-4o baselines and achieves performance comparable to human art students in both perceived originality and harmony. Additionally, a quantitative study shows that our method produces more diverse outputs and explores a broader conceptual space than its GPT-4o counterpart, demonstrating that artificial cultural alienness can unlock creative potential in autonomous agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Proceedings of the 39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025). Creative AI Track. 26 pages, 24 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20849","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:40:51Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:40:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:40:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":140,"totalPages":2,"page":2},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-26T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-27T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29%29"}}